Navigate to the Edit User functionality, select the Admin user account, and save the user details.
Capture the HTTP request generated by the Edit User functionality using an intercepting proxy such as Burp Suite. Observe that the password parameter is set to null in the request.
Modify the password parameter from null to a password of your choice and set the assignedRoleNames parameter to admin. Forward the modified request and observe that the Admin user's password is successfully updated.
Log in to the modified Admin user account using the newly assigned password. Observe that authentication is successful and access to the Admin account is granted.

It was observed that the application provides an Edit User functionality. An internal user with access to this functionality can change the password of another user's account by modifying the password parameter from null to an attacker-controlled password in the HTTP request. This indicates that an unauthorized user can modify another user's password without the account owner's consent, potentially resulting in account takeover.

An attacker with access to the affected user-management functionality can change the password of other user accounts and subsequently authenticate to those accounts.

Implement strict server-side authorization checks before allowing a user's password to be modified.
Ensure that users cannot change another user's password through the standard Edit User functionality unless explicitly authorized to perform this action.
Use a dedicated, secure password-reset workflow for administrative password changes.
Do not rely on client-controlled parameters such as password to determine whether a password change is permitted.
